.TH CHTTPSVR_ENGINE_STOP 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_engine_stop \- signal the engine to stop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

void chttpsvr_engine_stop(void);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Signals the shared reactor of chttpserver, and the idle-timeout sweep
thread beside it, to stop, forcing them down even while other
chttpsvr instances in the same process still hold a reference and are
still fully started.  This reactor is separate from the engine of
chttpclient, so a call to this function has no effect on the async
work of chttpclient in the same process (chttpclient_do_async,
chttpclient_do_async_streaming, chttpclient_do_pooled and
chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming).  It returns at once; use
chttpsvr_engine_wait() to block until the stop completes.

Before it tears the shared reactor down, the library quiesces every
chttpsvr instance that is still registered in the process, whether or
not the instance is still started, in exactly the way that
chttpsvr_destroy() does: it closes the listener, drains the in-flight
requests, closes the idle and parked connections and the requests
that wait for body memory, answers the queued streaming requests with
503, shuts the worker pools down, and frees the engine reference of
that instance.
This is what makes the ONE chttpsvr_destroy(srv) call in the
signal-handler shutdown pattern below safe, even though it runs after
the library has torn the engine down: srv has already been quiesced,
so that call only frees the remaining memory of the server and
repeats no teardown work.  This mechanism does not make repeated destroy
calls on the same handle safe: a second chttpsvr_destroy(srv) call on a
handle that an earlier call has already destroyed is a fatal error, with
abort() and SIGABRT, on this path as on every other one.

    static void on_signal(int sig) { (void)sig; chttpsvr_engine_stop(); }
    /* ... */
    if (chttpsvr_start(srv, &cfg) != ccol_success) {
        chttpsvr_destroy(srv);
        chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* the stop can be incomplete */
        return 1;
    }
    chttpsvr_engine_wait();   /* returns once on_signal fires */
    chttpsvr_destroy(srv);    /* safe: srv was already quiesced above */

This function does not block and is async-signal-safe, so it can be
called directly from a POSIX signal handler, for example for SIGINT or
SIGTERM.  It is the only function in this module that is documented
as async-signal-safe; chttpsvr_stop() is not (see the WARNINGS section
of that function), so do not call chttpsvr_stop() directly from a
signal handler.

The library installs no signal handlers; an application must connect
this function to whatever signal or shutdown mechanism it uses.

It is safe to call this function before the reactor has ever started;
it then does nothing.

You can call it more than once, including while the drain started by
an earlier call is still running, as happens when two SIGTERMs arrive
moments apart or when the shutdown code of an application makes an
extra defensive call.  A repeated or overlapping call does nothing and
is never a second teardown.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3),
.BR chttpsvr_stop (3),
